p3. same exact input, do the two committed radix arms answer differently?

arms  : quantised_add at <R=2,P=24>  and  <R=10,P=7>, the exact
        instantiations quantiser-radix2 and quantiser-radix10 run
input : both operands (neg=false, mag in [10^6, 10^7), exp = 0)
        at exp = 0 the triple denotes the integer `mag` under EITHER
        radix, so both arms receive the identical pair of reals and
        the exact sum they must round is the same integer
cmp   : exact rational, i128, no float anywhere in the comparison

trials                        : 200000
answers IDENTICAL             : 92556  (46.2780%)
answers DIFFERENT             : 107444  (53.7220%)

accuracy against the exact integer sum:
  binary32 exact              : 193747  (96.8735%)
  decimal32 exact             : 91329  (45.6645%)
  binary32 strictly closer    : 106167
  decimal32 strictly closer   : 0
  equal error                 : 93833

  5315940 + 8535628 = 13851568 exactly;  binary32 -> (13851568, 0),  decimal32 -> (1385157, 1)
  7250501 + 6768206 = 14018707 exactly;  binary32 -> (14018707, 0),  decimal32 -> (1401871, 1)
  9306627 + 9137899 = 18444526 exactly;  binary32 -> (9222263, 1),  decimal32 -> (1844453, 1)
  8522019 + 4286920 = 12808939 exactly;  binary32 -> (12808939, 0),  decimal32 -> (1280894, 1)
  9826539 + 9144143 = 18970682 exactly;  binary32 -> (9485341, 1),  decimal32 -> (1897068, 1)
  8548389 + 9261419 = 17809808 exactly;  binary32 -> (8904904, 1),  decimal32 -> (1780981, 1)

READING
  Both arms saw the identical pair of exact integers. Any nonzero
  DIFFERENT count is therefore two committed arms of one committed
  family returning different answers for one input, which is an
  accuracy difference and not an input-interpretation artefact.
  The `binary32 strictly closer` and `decimal32 strictly closer`
  counts are the ranking a fidelity coordinate would record, and
  no column of the committed CSV corpus carries it.
